          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Bohr topos
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            associated with any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+probability+theory"} quantum probability theory
          %p are equivalently
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
                and classical
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probability theory
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
                to the Bohr topos, hence in the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                of the Bohr topos.
          %p
            A detailed motivation/derivation of this from classical theorems on the foundations of standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            is at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order-theoretic+structure+in+quantum+mechanics"} order-theoretic structure in quantum mechanics
          %p
            One might think of a Bohr topos as (part of) a formalization of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordination"}> coordination
            ” of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+theory"} physical theory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            about (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"}> experimental
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            of the system. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of Bohr toposes has been argued (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#HeunenLandsmanSpitters09"} Heunen-Landsman-Spitters 09
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            going back to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann"} von Neumann
          %p
            The idea of Bohr toposes goes back to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ButterfieldHamiltonIsham"} Butterfield-Hamilton-Isham
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#IshamDoering07"} Isham-Döring 07
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HeunenLandsmanSpitters09"} Heunen-Landsman-Spitters 09
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niels+Bohr"} Niels Bohr
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics#Bohr49"} Bohr 1949
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Scheibe73"} Scheibe 1973, p. 24
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics#BohrStandpoint"} interpretation of quantum mechanics – Bohr’s standpoint
          %p
            Sometimes in the literature the discussion of Bohr toposes is referred to as “the topos-theoretic formulation of physics”. But actually Bohr toposes currently formalize but one aspect of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            a certain presheaf of Bohr toposes on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            does encode dynamics (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten11"}> Nuiten 11
            ). For other and more comprehensive usages of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            in the formalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory+and+physics"} higher category theory and physics
          %h3#IdeaInBrief Brief
          %p
            To every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            is associated its
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Bohr topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topos
            which plays the role of the quantum
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %em Bohrification
            – is that it naturally captures the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logical
            aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            in terms of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
          %p
            One way to understand Bohrification is as a generalization of the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+spectrum"} Gelfand spectrum
            of commutative
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            %em noncommutative
            = succeed "-algebras." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            essentially a system of Gelfand dual spaces to each of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+C%2A-algebra"} commutative
            subalgebras. Together, this system yields a generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+spectrum"} Gelfand spectrum
            in the form of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ̲
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{\Sigma}_A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_A
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_A \to \mathcal{C}(A)
            regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+category"} over
            the locale of open subalgebras.
          %p
            As a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topos
            with the original non-commutative algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            appearing as a commutative
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to the Bohr topos. This allows to talk about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            much like classical states, but internal to the Bohr topos.
          %p
            In fact, under mild assumptions on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            which knows about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            themselves. In order to have the Bohr topos remember the full non-commutative algebra structure of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flows"} flows
            induced on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            by automorphisms of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i [H,-])
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-]
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutator
            (that gets discarded as one passes to the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+algebra"} Jordan algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+D%C3%B6ring"} Andreas Döring
            (private communication at MPI Bonn, April 2013), this can be formulated nicely in topos theory.
          %h3#more_detailed More detailed
          %p
            The formalization of the notion
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            (see there for details) with its
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            is the following.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The system as such is encoded by a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+operator"} self-adjoint operator
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
                is thought of as being an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
                of the system: a kind of observation that one can make about it;
            %li
              %p
                a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℂ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : A \to \mathbb{C}
                (which is “positive” and “normalized”) is thought of as being a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
                that the system can be in – a physical configuration (or rather: a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+distribution"} probability distribution
                of such);
            %li
              %p
                the pairing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a, \rho) \mapsto \rho(a) \in \mathbb{C}
                is thought of as being the value of the observable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                made on the system in state
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ρ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
                of the system as measured in some chosen
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            %li
              %p
                a one-parameter
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo →
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to Aut(A)
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+automorphisms"} inner automorphisms
                for “localized” systems, see below) is thought of as being an
                %em evolution
                of the system, for instance in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
                or more general in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            (Often in the literature quantum mechanical systems are instead dually conceived of in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+states"} pure states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GNS-construction"} GNS-construction
            which allows to pass from one to the other.)
          %p
            Notice that these axioms are naturally thought of as exhibiting the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} formal dual
            of the would-be
            %em
              quantum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of the physical system – not quite an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            (which by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality"} Gelfand duality
            it would be if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            were commutative) but still a kind of generalized space. Traditionally it is common to regard this as a space in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec A \in C^\ast Alg^{op}
            that is the incarnation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in C^\ast Alg
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            – then the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            above reduces to the evident notion of real-valued functions on quantum phase space: such a function is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec A \to \mathbb{R}
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast Alg^{op}
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\mathbb{R})_0 \to A
            (where on the left we have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitization"} unitization
            of functions with
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compact support
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+operator"} self-adjoint operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the statement of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+calculus"} functional calculus
            for
            = succeed "-algebras." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
          %p
            More subtle is the interpretation of the axiom for
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+theorem"} spectral theorem
            two different
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1, a_2 \in A
            have a compatible set of observable values if and only if these elements commute with each other in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a_i\}_i
            of observables has a jointly consistent set of observable values if and only if the sub-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            \-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \{a_i\}_i\rangle \subset A
            generated by them is commutative. Therefore for the phenomenological interpretation of the axioms it seems to make no sense to demand that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : A \to \mathbb{C}
            be linear on non-commuting observables: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_2
            do not commute, it is not a-priori clear that it makes sense to require that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(a_1 + a_2) = \rho(a_1) + \rho(a_2)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1, a_2
          %p
            Therefore the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-state"} quasi-state
            was introduced: a quasi-state on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is defined to be a (positive and normalized)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : A \to \mathbb{C}
            which is required to be
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear
            only on all commutative subalgebras of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            One would therefore tend to think that the terminology has been chosen in an unfortunate way. While maybe true, it turns out – non-trivially – that in a major class of cases of interest the distinction does not matter: namely
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            states that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            a separable complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dim
                  %mi H
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim H \gt 2
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = B(H)
            the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operators"} bounded operators
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            are automatically states: a function that is linear on all commutative subalgebras is automatically also linear on all of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p While this means that the distinction between states and quasi-states disappears in a major case of interest, it does not disappear in all cases of interest. In particular, other foundational theorems about quantum mechanics concern the collection of commutative subalgebras, too.
          %p
            Notably, one may wonder about the evident strengthening of the notion of quasi-states to that of a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : A \to \mathbb{C}
            which is not just linear but also an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            on each commuting subalgebra. Notice that by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality"} Gelfand duality
            every commutative
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the algebra of continuous functions on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sp(C)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+distribution"} probability distribution
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sp(C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to \mathbb{C}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            may be thought of as encoding a collection of precise numerical values (as opposed to just expectation values) of
            %em all
            possible observables. Such a hypothetical quasi-state is sometimes called a collection of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+variable"}> hidden variable
              s
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kochen-Specker+theorem"} Kochen-Specker theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            work, there is
            %em no
            quasi-state which is commutative-subalgebra-wise an algebra homomorphism.
          %p In summary, this means:
          .standout
            %p
              The foundational characteristics of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
              are encoded in notions of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              which are linear and positive only
              %em commutative-subalgebra-wise
              \.
          %p
            Since the notion of
            %strong commutative-subalgebra-wise homomorphism
            is at the heart of quantum physics, it seems worthwhile to consider natural formalizations of this notion. There is indeed a very natural and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} general abstract
            one: whenever any notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            is defined only
            %em locally
            it is natural to consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of such functions over all possible local patches.
          %p
            The historically motivating example, and possibly still the most widely familiar one, is that of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+function"} holomorphic function
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of functions on the complex manifold.
          %p There is an evident analog setup of this situation that applies in the present case of interest, that of functions defined on commutative subalgebras:
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(A)
            for the set of all its commutative
            = succeed "-subalgebras." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            under inclusion of subalgebras. A (co)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            of this set is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(A) \to Set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{C}(A), Set]
            – is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf topos
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} Inside
            this
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %p
            First of all, the non-commutative
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            naturally induces a
            %em commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi A
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{A}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{C}(A), Set]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaf"} copresheaf
            defined by the tautological assignment
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi A
                    %mo ̲
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underline{A} : (C \in \mathcal{C}(A)) \mapsto C
                  \\,.
          %p
            In words this means nothing but that the collection of all commutative subalgebras of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            may naturally be regarded as a
            %em single
            commutative
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathcal{C}(A), Set]
          %p
            Below we shall discuss (
            %a{:href => "..."}> here
            ) that in a precise sense this commutative internal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi A
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{A}
            captures precisely all the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematics"} kinematical
            information encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            – everything related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            but not information about (time) evolution. So everything we have discussed so far.
          %p The pair of these two ingredients
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bohr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %munder
                    %mi A
                    %mo ̲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Bohr(A) := ([\mathcal{C}(A), Set], \underline{A})
          %p
            constitutes what is called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topos
            – a special case of the notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+topos"} locally ringed topos
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"}> Lurie09
            ) is based on modelling spaces as ringed toposes.
          %p
            We shall call this ringed topos the
            %strong Bohr topos
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            This terminology is meant to indicate that one may think of this construction as formalizing faithfully and usefully a heuristic that has been emphasized by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niels+Bohr"} Niels Bohr
            – one of the founding fathers of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            – and is known as the
            %em doctrine of classical concepts
            (
            %a{:href => "#Scheibe73"}> Scheibe 1973
            ) in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            of quantum mechanics may be, it is to be probed and detected by classical/commutative logic/geometry.
          %p
            Namely in terms of the Bohr topos we have the following equivalent reformulations of the foundational facts about quantum physics discussed above, now internally in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Bohr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bohr(A)
          .standout
            %p
              %strong Consistent quantum mechanical states.
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-state"} quasi-state
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is precisely an ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+state"} classical state
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mi A
                        %mo ̲
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{A}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Bohr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bohr(A)
            %p
              In particular (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"}> Gleason's theorem
              ): if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = B(H)
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi dim
                    %mi H
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim H \gt 2
              then a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
              on the external
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is precisely a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+state"} classical state
              on the internal
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mi A
                        %mo ̲
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{A}
          %p and
          .standout
            %p
              %strong Non-existence of hidden quantum variables.
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+spectrum"} Gelfand spectrum
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mi A
                      %mo ̲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sp(\underline{A})
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi A
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{A}
              internal to the Bohr topos has no
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"} global point
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kochen-Specker+theorem"} Kochen-Specker
          %p
            These two statements might be taken as suggesting that a quantum mechanical system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is naturally regarded in terms of its Bohr topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bohr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bohr(A)
            – somewhat more naturally than as a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ButterfieldHamiltonIsham"} IshamHamiltonButterfield
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => ""} Spitters
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topos
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bohr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bohr(A)
            itself
            %em is
            the
            %em
              quantum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of the quantum mechanical system in question.
          %p
            To which extent this perspective is genuinely useful is maybe still to be established. For pointers to the literature see the
            %a{:href => "#References"} references
            below. Discussion along the above lines may suggest that this perspective is indeed useful, but what is probably still missing is a statement about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            that can be formulated and proven in terms of Bohr toposes, while being hardly conceivable or at least more unnatural without. It is probably currently not clear if such statements have been found.
          %p
            One potential such statement has been suggested in (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten11"}> Nuiten 11
            ) after discussion with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bas+Spitters"} Spitters
          %p
            In the formalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            – called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            – every quantum field theory is entirely encoded in terms of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaf"} copresheaf
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A : Op(X) \to C^\ast Alg
          %p
            which assigns to every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            the quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsystem"} subsystem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_U
            of quantum fields supported in that region. By the above, we may consider for each of these quantum systems their associated quantum
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Bohr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bohr(A_U)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bohr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi com
                    %mo *
                  %mi TopSpace
                  %mo ↪
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi com
                    %mo *
                  %mi Topos
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Bohr(A) : Op(X)^{op} \to C^\ast_{com} TopSpace \hookrightarrow C^\ast_{com}Topos
          %p
            of ringed toposes whose internal ring object has the structure of a commutative
            = succeed "-algebra." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaf"} copresheaf
            thus turned into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            it is natural to ask under which conditions this is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten11"}> Nuiten 11
            ) the following is observed: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            satisfies what is called the
            %em split property
            (a strong form of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time+slice+axiom"} time slice axiom
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+geometric+morphism"} local geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi Op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A : Op(X) \to C^\ast Alg
            is indeed
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local
            – spatially and causally. So this means that a natural property of quantum physics – spatial and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
            – corresponds from the perspective of Bohr toposes to a natural property of presheaves of quantum phase spaces:
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
          %p One can probably view this as further suggestive evidence that indeed quantum physics is naturally regarded from the point of view of the Bohr topos. But for seeing where this perspective is headed, it seems that more insights along these lines would be useful.
          %h2#outline Outline
          %p
            The discussion below proceeds in the following steps (following (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten11"}> Nuiten 11
            ))
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#BohrToposOfQMSystem"} Bohr topos of a quantum mechanics system
              %p
                This discusses the Bohr topos incarnation of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
                – the topos-theoretic quantum
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
                – and its
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functoriality
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#KinematicsOnBohrTopos"} Kinematics in a Bohr topos
              %p
                This discusses how the classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematics"} kinematics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
                a Bohr topos is the external quantum kinematics of the underlying quantum mechanical system.
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#SheafOfBohrToposesOfQFT"} (Pre-)Sheaf of Bohr toposes of a quantum field theory
              %p
                This discusses how the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
                of Bohr toposes obtained by applying Bohrification to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
                of a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %h2#BohrToposOfQMSystem Bohr topos of a quantum mechanical system
          %p
            We discuss the definitions and some basic properties of Bohr toposes: certain
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+spaces"} ringed spaces
            – associated with any (possibly non-commutative) algebra. We formulate the construction for
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} star-algebra
            structure until we come to the discussion of the
            %a{:href => "KinematicsOnBohrTopos"} kinematics in a Bohr topos
            below.
          %h3#algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            \-algebras
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
              is (…)
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} star-algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
              is called a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+operator"} normal element
              if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mo *
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^* a = a a^*
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Every element of a
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi a
                        %mo +
                        %msup
                          %mi a
                          %mo *
                        %mo )
                      %mo +
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi a
                        %mo −
                        %msup
                          %mi a
                          %mo *
                        %mo )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    a = \frac{1}{2} \left( \left( a + a^* \right) + \left( a - a^*  \right) \right)
                    \\,.
            %p
              This means whenever a linear morphism between the vector spaces underlying two
              = succeed "-algebras" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
          #PartialCStar.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                partial
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                equipped with
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a symmetric and reflexive binary
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \subset A \times A
              %li
                %p
                  elements
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0,1 \in A
              %li
                %p
                  an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mo →
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast : A \to A
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo :
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mo ×
                          %mi A
                          %mo →
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\cdot (-) : \mathbb{C} \times A \to A
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mo −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mo →
                          %mi ℝ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vert-\Vert : A \to \mathbb{R}
              %li
                %p
                  (partial) binary operations
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ×
                        %mo :
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +, \times : C \to A
            %p
              such that every set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset A
              of elements that are pairwise in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is contained in a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \subset A
              whose elements are also pairwise in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and on which the above operations yield the structure of a commutative
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              of partial
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PCstar
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PCstar
              of partial
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
              algebras.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#vdBergHeunen"}> vdBergHeunen, def. 11,12
            ).
          #PartialAlgebraOfNormalOperators.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi a
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mo *
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    N(A) := \{a \in A | a a^* = a^* a\}
            %p
              for its set of
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+operator"} normal operator
              %a{:href => "#PartialCStar"} partial C-star algebra
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \subset N(A) \times N(A)
              the set of pairs of elements that commute in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#BohrSite The Bohr site
          #PosetOfCommutativeSubalgebras.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a
              %a{:href => "#PartialCStar"} partial C-star algebra
              write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(A)
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              of total (not partial) commutative sub
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p This construction extends to a functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                    %mo →
                    %mi Poset
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{C} : C^* Alg \to Poset
                    \\,.
            %p
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poset"} Poset
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
              a homomorphism we let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(f)
              be over any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \in \mathcal{C}(A)
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi im
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(f|_C) \in C^\ast Alg
              of the restriction of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              By standard properties of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/C-star-algebra#General"} here
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi im
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(f|_C)
              is simply the set-theoretic image
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(C)
          %p
            Notice the following fact about
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandroff+space"} Alexandroff space
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p The functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Alex
                    %mo :
                    %mi Poset
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Alex : Poset \to Top
            %p
              from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              that sends a poset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              to the topological space whose underlying set is the underlying set of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              and whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              are the upward closed subsets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Up
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Up(P)
              exhibits an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Poset
                    %munderover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi Alex
                    %mi AlexTop
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Poset
                    \\underoverset{\simeq}{Alex}{\to}
                    AlexTop
                    \\hookrightarrow
                    Top
            %p
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poset"} Poset
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              of
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandroff+space"} Alexandroff space
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in C^* Alg
              we call
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Alex
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alex \mathcal{C}(A)
              the
              %strong
                Bohr
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^* Alg
              is called
              %strong commutativity reflecting
              if for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1, a_2 \in A
              we have that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a_1)
              commutes with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a_2)
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              then already
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_1
              commutes with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_2
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi cr
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C^* Alg_{cr} \subset C^* Alg
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
              of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow B
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^* Alg
              is commutativity reflecting.
          #CommutativityReflectionByAdjoints.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^* Alg
              is commutativity reflecting precisely if the morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(f)
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mi f
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathcal{C}(f) \dashv R_f)
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathcal{C}(A)
                    \\stackrel{\overset{R_f}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{\mathcal{C}(f)}{\to}}
                    \\mathcal{C}(B)
                    \\,.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten11"}> Nuiten 11, lemma 2.6
            ).
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_8 Definition
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi inc
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi cr
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C^* Alg_{inc}
                    \\subset
                    C^* Alg_{cr}
                    \\subset
                    C^* Alg
            %p
              for the subcategories of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^* Alg
              on the monomorphisms and on the commutativity-reflecting morphisms, respectively.
          %h3#BohrTopos The Bohr topos
          #CStarTopos.num_defn
            %h6#definition_9 Definition
            %p Write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi com
                      %mo *
                    %mi Top
                    %mo ↪
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi com
                      %mo *
                    %mi Topos
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C^*_{com} Top \hookrightarrow C^*_{com} Topos
            %p
              for the categories of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+spaces"} ringed spaces
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+toposes"} ringed toposes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              ring object is equipped with the structure of an internal
              %em commutative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
              and the morphisms respect the
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %p
              Hence a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ,
                    %munder
                      %mi A
                      %mo ̲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mo ,
                    %munder
                      %mi B
                      %mo ̲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{E}, \underline{A}) \to (\mathcal{F}, \underline{B})
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi com
                      %mo *
                    %mi Topos
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast_{com} Topos
              is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi ℰ
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℱ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{F}
                  such that the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                        %munder
                          %mi B
                          %mo ̲
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^* \underline{B}
                  is still an internal
                  = succeed "-algebra" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℰ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              %li
                %p
                  and a morphism of internal
                  = succeed "-algebras" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                        %munder
                          %mi B
                          %mo ̲
                        %mo →
                        %munder
                          %mi A
                          %mo ̲
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        f^\ast \underline{B} \to \underline{A}
                %p
                  in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℰ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          #TheSheafTopos.num_defn
            %h6#definition_10 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in C^* Alg
              the
              %strong Bohr topos
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is the
              = succeed "-space/topos" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
